OOPs-Object Oriented Programming Language
  1. Encapsulation:
      - Encapsulation is the process of wrapping data (variables) and methods into a single unit called a class. It is also used to achieve data hiding.
  2. Inheritance:
      - Inheritance is the process of acquiring the properties and methods of one class into another class.
  3. Polymorphism
      - Polymorphism means one thing having many forms. It allows a method or object to behave in different ways.
  4. Abstraction
      - Abstraction is the process of hiding implementation details and showing only essential information to the user.


**Inheritance in Java (OOPs)**
Definition: Inheritance is the process of acquiring the properties (variables) and methods of one class into another class. It is used to achieve code reusability.
- Parent Class: The class whose properties are inherited.
- Child Class: The class that inherits the properties of another class.
- Keyword: extends
Types of Inheritance in Java
There are five types of inheritance in OOPs:
1. Single Inheritance
    -When one child class inherits the properties and methods from only one parent class, it is called single inheritance.
    Syntax:
    class A {
        // Variables and methods
    }
    class B extends A {
        // Variables and methods
    }

2. Multilevel Inheritance
    -When a class inherits from another class, and a third class inherits from that child class, it is called multilevel inheritance.
    Syntax:
    class A {
        // Variables and methods
    }
    class B extends A {
        // Variables and methods
    }
    class C extends B {
        // Variables and methods
    }

3. Hierarchical Inheritance:
    -When multiple child classes inherit the properties and methods from a single parent class, it is called hierarchical inheritance.
    Syntax:
    class A {
        // Variables and methods
    }
    class B extends A {
        // Variables and methods
    }
    class C extends A {
        // Variables and methods
    }

4. Multiple Inheritance
    -When one child class inherits the properties and methods from more than one parent class, it is called multiple inheritance.
    Syntax (using interfaces in Java):
    interface A {
        // Methods
    }
    interface B {
        // Methods
    }   
    class C implements A, B {
        // Implement methods
    }
Note: Java does not support multiple inheritance through classes, but it supports multiple inheritance through interfaces.

5. Hybrid Inheritance
    -The combination of two or more types of inheritance is called hybrid inheritance.
    Syntax (using interfaces in Java):
    class A {
        // Variables and methods
    }
    interface B {
        // Methods
    }
    class C extends A implements B {
        // Implement methods
    }
Note: Hybrid inheritance involving multiple parent classes is not directly supported through Java classes. It can be achieved using interfaces.
